{"text": "\u2018American Journal of Bpidemilogy\n\nepyrag 6 8 by The Jobe Hopkin Univerity Scho! of Hygiene and Puc Heath\n\n\u2018ilo reered|\n\nVol 195, No\nPrinted n USA\n\nBrief Reports\n\nRELIABILITY OF SURROGATE INFORMATION ON CIGARETTE\nSMOKING BY TYPE OF INFORMANT\n\nJOSEPH K. McLAUGHLIN* MICHAEL 8. DIETZ? ERIC 8. MEHL? aNp WILLIAM J. BLOT\u2019\n\n\u2018Surrogate interviews are often used in\nepidemiologic research, particularly in eti\nlogic investigations of rapidly fatal di\neases. The reliability of surrogate inform-\nants for cigarette smoking information has\nbeen reported in a number of studies (1-7).\n\u2018Often these studies are of living husband-\nwife pairs interviewed separately (4-7), al-\nthough some of the surrogates were inter-\nviewed after the death of the original study\nsubject (1-3). This brief report compares\nsurrogate- and self-responses on cigarette\nsmoking obtained approximately 10 years\nafter original interviews of study subjects.\nIn an attempt to provide further insight\ninto the reliability of surrogate respond-\nents, we have analyzed results by type of\nsurrogate and sex of the deceased study\nsubjects.\n\nMATERIALS AND METHODS\nThe data used in this analysis were ob:\ntained from the epidemiologic follow-up of\nthe first National Health and Nutrition\nExamination Survey (NHANES 1) (8).\nNHANES I was a detailed study of adults\n25 to 74 years of age randomly selected\nduring the period 1971-1975. Interviews\nwith surviving cohort members were con-\nducted in 1982-1984. Deaths among mem-\nbers of this cohort during 1971-1984 were\nascertained, and interviews sought with the\nnext of kin of those deceased. The interview\n\nReceived for publication September 4, 1985, and in\nfinal form December 24, 1986\n\" Biowatatics Branch, Division of Cancer Biology,\nNationel Cancer Intute, Lando, 9C18, Bethesda,\nMb 20802\n* West, Inc, Rockile, MD.\n\nwstrument included questions on demo-\n\u2018graphic background, family history, smok-\ning, alcohol consumption, and the circum-\nstances of death of the decedent. Few of the\n\u2018questions in the surrogate follow-up ques-\ntionnaire were exactly the same as in the\nearlier questionnaire. For cigarette smok-\ning, however, questions on smoking status\n\u2018and age when smoking began were similar\nbetween the two periods, and responses\n\u2018were analyzed for original versus follow-up\ncomparability. Of the 14,407 adults in the\noriginal 1971-1975 survey, 6,913 were ad-\nministered questions on cigarette smoking.\n\u2018Among these, 5,107 of 5,469 living subjects\nand 702 surrogates of 831 deceased subjects\n\u2018were interviewed in the follow-up survey.\n\u2018The remaining 613 original subjects were\neither lost to follow-up or incapacitated and\nnot included in this analysis.\n\nResuuts\n\u2018Table 1 presents the per cent agreement\nfor cigarette smoking status, as defined by\nhaving smoked 100 cigarettes or more, be-\ntween self-reports in the original 1971-1975\nsurvey and surrogate- and self-reports in\n1982-1984. Correct identification of smok-\ning status was generally provided by most\ntypes of surrogates, except for siblings of\nmale decedents. The level of agreement for\nsurrogates combined, ranging from 85-95\nper cent, was remarkably similar to that of\nthe living subjects, particularly for the sur-\nrogates of deceased females.\nData among smokers for mean age when\ncigarette smoking began for surrogate- and\nself-respondents are presented in table 2.\nFor males, but not females, responding sur\n\n256S18S202",
